  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe Bermuda High builds into the Eastern Seaboard through the \nweekend, bringing building heat and humidity possibly extending into \nearly next week. This brings an extended period of seasonably warm \ntemperatures, combined with anomalously high dew points.\n\nTemperatures themselves won't be anything remarkable, with \nwidespread highs in the upper 80s to low 90s across much of the \narea. Additionally, an afternoon sea breeze is likely along the \ncoastline both Thursday and Friday, but then looks confined to the \nMidCoast for the weekend.\n\nThe dew points themselves will be the bigger story, with dew points \npushing into the low 70s starting on Thursday across southern New \nHampshire. These dew points continue to expand, and push into the \nlow to mid 70s for much of the area from Friday through at least \nSunday.\n\nIncreasingly, it looks like a stronger front that brings a return to \ncooler conditions takes longer to arrive, with next Tuesday to \nWednesday looking like the more likely timeframe for a more \nsubstantial cool down. Before this, a couple of fronts make some \nprogress into New England, but gradually wash out without bringing a \nnoticeable cool down. One such front is likely on Saturday, and\nthe clouds, showers and storms associated with it may serve as \na spoiler for the higher temperatures expected on the other days\naround it.\n\nWith the heat and humidity building, a Heat Advisory has been \nissued for parts of southern New Hampshire on Thursday as heat \nindices push into the mid to upper 90s. It likely takes until \nFriday to see more widespread heat indices in the mid 90s push \ninto the rest of the interior forecast area just back from the \ncoastline.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION... \n\nNew England will be positioned on the northwest flank of a sub \ntropical ridge Thursday through Friday with a long wave trough \nto the west over the central US. Short waves moving through the\ntrough will help suppress the ridge by Saturday with Saturday\nhaving the highest chances for showers and storms through the\nperiod.\n\nDeep southwest flow will advect anomalous moisture into the\nregion Thursday with this moisture persisting into Saturday. Dew\npoints will climb into the upper 60s across much of the area by\nThursday afternoon with southern NH and SW Maine seeing dew \npoints into the low 70s. PWATs will also climb above 1.75 inches\nacross much of the area with pockets of PWATs greater than 2\ninches. Surface heating combined with areas of dew points in the\n70s will yield upwards of 2000 J/kg of SB CAPE amid 25-30 kts of\ndeep layer shear Thursday afternoon. The proximity to the ridge\nand lack of surface forcing will limit chances for an isolated\nstorm across New Hampshire Thursday afternoon. Given the CAPE\nand shear a strong storm cannot be ruled out and high PWATs\ncombined with deep warm cloud depths would allow for torrential\nrainfall. Friday will be similar with the ridge close by and\nlack of surface forcing while expanding high dew points will\nallow for sufficient instability for storms across much of the \narea. Shear will be somewhat weaker around 25 kts or less and\nCAMs show little in the way of storm coverage. Given sufficient\nSB CAPE with no CIN have largely stuck with NBM PoPs Friday\nafternoon which are around 20-30 percent. High PWATs and deep\nwarm cloud depths will again allow for any thunderstorm that\ndoes develop be prolific rain producers.\n\nThe 12Z model suite is in general agreement that short waves\nwill start to suppress the ridge Friday night into Saturday.\nThese waves will continue chances for showers and storms Friday\nnight into Saturday morning. Forcing for ascent remains\nfavorable through Saturday evening while cloud cover brings into\nquestion how much the atmosphere can destabilize for storms. \nModels generally suggest there will be sufficient CAPE and shear\nfor storms with the continued theme of high PWATs and deep warm\ncloud depths allowing for storms to produce torrential \nrainfall. Shear will also be a bit stronger so instability\ntrends will need to be watched for potential strong to severe\nstorms. Daily chances for showers and storms diminish Sunday\ninto Monday as flow aloft turns more zonal.",
